A first exact computation shows why “ramification is effective” is not yet the budget: for an exceptional divisor \(E\), the resolved coefficient is
\[
r_E=(\beta-1)v_E(F)+(\alpha-1)v_E(G)+(N+d+e-3)v_E(Z)+k_E-3m_E,
\]
with \(m_E=\min\{\beta v_E(F),\alpha v_E(G),Nv_E(Z)\}\). This is useful because it exposes the only possible numerical leverage—but I’m checking signs and conventions carefully before banking it.
